<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/network-radio-wakeups.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">Read receipts arrive one by one, each triggering a tiny request. The bytes look small, but the app keeps asking the network to work. A freshness requirement is needed before deciding what can wait for a batch.</p>

Separate immediate user actions from deferrable delivery, then make queue age, retries and network conditions explicit.

<!-- @after Batching :: visual -->

### A batch is a freshness policy

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Enqueue</strong><small>Persist required events with stable identity.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Wait within policy</strong><small>Accumulate until a size, age or user-action trigger.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Deliver and reconcile</strong><small>Acknowledge completed work; retry safely within a bound.</small></div></div><figcaption>Conceptual queue flow. Batching can reduce separate transfers; actual radio residency and energy depend on network/device behavior and must be measured.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Retry Policy :: animation -->

### An uncertain delivery must not become a retry storm

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">An uncertain delivery must not become a retry storm</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="queue" data-art="queue" data-art-role="queue"><strong>Queue</strong><small>Required event persisted</small></div><div class="node" data-el="send" data-art="cloud" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Attempt</strong><small>Response may be lost</small></div><div class="node" data-el="retry" data-art="clock" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Retry policy</strong><small>Cap, delay, idempotency</small></div><div class="node" data-el="done" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Acknowledgement</strong><small>Remove reconciled work</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
  {
    "label": "Enqueue",
    "caption": "A required event enters durable storage. Its identity lets a later attempt refer to the same business operation.",
    "state": {
      "queue": "active"
    },
    "add": [
      "queue",
      "send",
      "retry",
      "done"
    ],
    "visuals": {
      "queue": {
        "count": 1
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Lose the response",
    "caption": "The request may reach the server even if the client receives no acknowledgement. This illustrative failure leaves the result uncertain.",
    "state": {
      "queue": "done",
      "send": "warn"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Bound recovery",
    "caption": "Use an idempotent operation or server-supported key, backoff and a retry limit. A connectivity change does not prove the endpoint will accept the request.",
    "state": {
      "retry": "active"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Reconcile",
    "caption": "Remove confirmed work and keep unresolved work according to policy. Track oldest age as well as queue depth.",
    "state": {
      "retry": "done",
      "done": "active"
    },
    "visuals": {
      "queue": {
        "count": 0
      }
    }
  }
]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Observability :: staff -->

Track the age of the oldest queued operation, acknowledged deliveries and bounded retry attempts alongside request volume. A batching change needs both an energy/network hypothesis and an acceptable freshness limit. Preserve operation identity across retries so uncertain delivery does not create duplicate side effects. Request count is only an application signal: claim fewer radio wakeups only when the chosen device evidence actually measures or supports that conclusion.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>A tiny request timed out. Is it safe to create a new write?</summary>

Not necessarily. The server may have committed it; use idempotency or reconcile before duplicating business work.

</details>

<details><summary>Why cap event age as well as batch size?</summary>

A quiet app may never fill a batch, leaving required events waiting indefinitely.

</details>

<details><summary>Does validated connectivity guarantee endpoint success?</summary>

No. Capabilities describe observed network state, which can change; individual requests still fail.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Network access](https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/network-ops/network-access-optimization), [Network capabilities](https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/network-ops/reading-network-state).
